@part id="part2" num="II" kind="PART TWO · ❖" title="The People/of the Text" sub="Almost everyone in this book has been flattened into a lesson by somebody who needed them to mean one thing. Go back and find out what they actually did."
toc: i · How to read a person | p2-method
toc: ii · The women of the Hebrew Bible | p2-women
toc: iii · The women of the New Testament | p2-ntwomen
toc: iv · The men | p2-men
toc: v · Who echoes whom | p2-cast

@essay id="p2-method" rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · METHOD"
# How to read a person
drop: Every profile in this part runs the same fields, and the order matters. It starts with what the person has been reduced to, because that reduction is what you inherited, and it'll keep steering your instincts until it's written down and looked at. The text comes next, only what's on the page, including the parts that are inconvenient for everyone.
After that, the Empire's use of them, because Dominion has a position on each of these people, and a state that quotes scripture is always curating a gallery of approved and cautionary lives. Then the echoes in your own cast, and most important, where the parallel breaks, because the break is where your character stops being a reference and starts being a person.
rec:
Usually reduced to | The received version: the sermon, the flannelgraph, the insult the name has become.
What the text gives | Only what's written, with the silences marked as silences and left unfilled.
What Dominion does | The Empire's official reading, and what it needs that reading to authorize.
TKC echoes | Which of your characters this lights up, and in which scene.
Where it breaks | The required field. No character is an allegory, and the difference is the character.
Questions and notes | Yours. Nothing printed here has any claim on it.
content:
side:
k: A note on the unnamed
stext: A great many women in this collection have no name: Lot's wife, Jephthah's daughter, the woman of Judges 19, the woman who bled for twelve years, the woman at the well, the woman who anointed him. Who got a name tells you who was doing the recording. The Registry makes the same choices.
k: People already worked
stext: Sarah, Rebekah, Leah, Dinah, Shiphrah and Puah, and Zipporah are worked in the Genesis and Exodus dossiers and cross-referenced here.
marg
see: Deep dives I and II; the cast-name table at the end of this part.
/end

@essay id="p2-women" rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · I"
# Eve
small: Genesis 2–4 · the woman of the garden · the first dialogue in the Bible
rec:
Usually reduced to | The woman who brought sin into the world.
What the text gives | Called *ishshah*, "woman," by the man, and named Ḥawwah, "mother of all living," only after the judgment (3.20). Her conversation with the serpent is the first dialogue in the Bible. She names her sons, and both times she speaks of God: "I have gotten a man with the LORD" (4.1), "God has appointed for me another offspring" (4.25). Genesis 3 doesn't curse her, and the word "sin" doesn't appear in it.
Where the blame came from | Later, and from outside the chapter. Sirach, about 180 BCE: "from a woman sin had its beginning." 1 Timothy 2.14: "Adam was not deceived, but the woman was." Tertullian, about 200 CE: "you are the devil's gateway." Paul's undisputed letters put it on Adam: "sin came into the world through one man" (Rom 5.12).
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: The Broadcast's Eve problem, the woman "blamed for asking rather than thanked" (TR), runs the man's defense from 3.12.
Where it breaks | Eve is never asked what she wanted. Eden is asked constantly, on camera, and graded on the answer.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill
side:
chips: OT, ORIGINS
k: Themes
stext: Asking · blame · naming · the first speech
marg
see: Deep dive I, Genesis, pages 2 and 4.
/end

@essay rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · II"
# Hagar
small: Genesis 16 · 21 · Egyptian · enslaved · the only person in scripture who names God
rec:
Usually reduced to | A subplot. The servant who caused trouble, the mistake made while waiting on a promise, the woman whose son wasn't chosen.
What the text gives | An enslaved foreign woman handed to her mistress's husband with no recorded consent and no line of dialogue until she's pregnant and being mistreated. She runs once. In the desert she's met, addressed by name, given a promise about her own descendants, and told to go back. When the chosen son is born she's expelled with bread and a skin of water, and she sets the boy under a bush because she can't watch him die. She's the one who names God, El-roi, the God who sees. Nobody else in Genesis does that. She later takes a wife for Ishmael from Egypt, head of her own household.
Afterlives | Paul makes her an allegory of slavery (Gal 4). Islamic tradition honors her as Hajar, and pilgrims at Mecca retrace her run between two hills. Delores Williams built womanist theology on her survival in *Sisters in the Wilderness* (1993).
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: Makes her a warning about the servant who forgets her place, and cites the order to return as proof God sends women back to the households they flee. Her naming of God isn't taught.
TKC echoes | Any woman whose body is the instrument of someone else's covenant. The candidate who's used and then surplus.
Where it breaks | Hagar is met in the wilderness and survives it twice. If your character runs and no one meets her, that's a different theology, and you should know which one you're writing.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill
side:
chips: OT, CHARACTER
k: Themes
stext: Surrogacy · exile · being seen · the used body
marg
see: Deep dive I, Genesis, page 8.
/end

@essay rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · III"
# Tamar
small: Genesis 38 · twice widowed · defrauded of her claim · declared righteous by the man who wronged her
rec:
Usually reduced to | A scandal skipped in public reading. If she's mentioned, she's the woman who pretended to be a prostitute.
What the text gives | Married into a family, widowed, married to the next brother by custom, widowed again, and sent back to her father's house while the family quietly keeps the third son from her. She's left in legal limbo, neither wife nor free, with no court to appeal to. She veils herself, waits on the road her father-in-law will travel, and takes his seal, cord and staff as a pledge. Accused and sentenced to burn, she sends the three items with a message: "Identify whose these are." He says she's more righteous than he is. Her son Perez enters the royal line.
How scripture remembers her | The elders bless Ruth's marriage, "may your house be like the house of Perez, whom Tamar bore to Judah" (Ruth 4.12), and Matthew names her in Jesus' genealogy (1.3).
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: Omits her. There's no usable version of a story in which a woman entraps a patriarch, is vindicated by the record and written into the ancestry of kings.
TKC echoes | Any woman with no legal standing who has to create evidence. She doesn't accuse. She collects.
Where it breaks | Tamar's society had a law on her side that wasn't being enforced. If Dominion's law is against your character, producing evidence won't be enough. She'll have to change the court.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill
side:
chips: OT, CHARACTER
k: Themes
stext: Legal limbo · evidence · veiling · vindication
marg
see: Deep dive I, Genesis, pages 7 and 8.
/end

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · IV" m="EXOD 2; EXOD 15.20–21; NUM 12; NUM 20.1–2; MIC 6.4; B. TAʿANIT 9A; TKC: MIRIAM · TL"
# Miriam
small: Exodus 2 · 15 · Numbers 12 · 20 · Micah 6.4 · the first woman the Torah calls a prophet
rec:
What the text gives | Moses' sister, if the unnamed girl at the river is her. Called "the prophet" at the sea, where she leads the women with a hand drum (Exod 15.20–21). She and Aaron challenge Moses' sole authority, and she alone is struck with a skin disease. The camp doesn't move until she's brought back (Num 12). She dies at Kadesh, and the next verse says there was no water (Num 20.1–2). The rabbis built on that the tradition of Miriam's well, which traveled with Israel for her sake. Micah names her with Moses and Aaron as the three God sent.
Her name | Miriam in Greek is Mariam, which is where every Mary in the New Testament gets hers.
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: Teaches Numbers 12 as the lesson on women who speak against ordained authority, and leaves out the song and the well.
TKC echoes | Eden's mother carries her name (TL). Miriam was reassigned to another man's household after Thomas left (FR 0), and Celeste has no proof she's alive (FR 16).
Where it breaks | The biblical camp waits for Miriam. Whether anyone in the Empire waits for this one is the question.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · V" m="JUDG 4–5; JUDG 5.1; JUDG 5.7; JUDG 5.28–30; TKC: DOM"
# Deborah
small: Judges 4–5 · prophet · judge · the problem no empire has solved
rec:
Usually reduced to | An exception. The woman God used because no man was available, a reading the text doesn't support anywhere.
What the text gives | Already a prophet and the judge of Israel when we meet her, hearing cases under a palm tree. No emergency is offered to excuse it, and no apology. CONTESTED: *ʾeshet lappidot* can mean "wife of Lappidoth" or "a woman of torches." She summons the commander Barak, who won't go unless she goes with him. She goes, and tells him the glory will go to a woman. It goes to Jael, a civilian who kills the enemy general with a tent peg. The victory song, among the oldest poetry in the Bible, calls her "a mother in Israel" and ends with Sisera's mother at her window imagining her son dividing the spoil, "a womb or two for every man."
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: Emergency doctrine. She's taught as a temporary provision for a lawless age, proof that when men fail God shames them by using a woman. Her authority becomes an insult aimed at men, and stops being a precedent for women.
TKC echoes | The woman with real authority who's constantly called an exception so the rule survives. And the general who'll only act if a woman stands beside him.
Where it breaks | Deborah is never punished, never diminished and never justifies herself. If your character's authority costs her something, the cost is your invention, and worth being honest about.
corr: The old companion said Deborah composed the song. The heading credits "Deborah and Barak" (5.1). The song speaks of her in the first person in places, and who composed it isn't stated.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · VI–VII" m="JOSH 2; JOSH 6.22–25; JOSEPHUS, ANT. 5.1.2; MATT 1.5; HEB 11.31; JAS 2.25; RUTH 1–4; RUTH 3.9; LEV 19.9–10; DEUT 23.3"
# Rahab · Ruth
lede: Two foreign women who talk their way into the royal genealogy.
rec:
Rahab · Joshua 2, 6 | **Reduced to:** her profession, permanently. A *zonah*, usually "prostitute" (Josephus calls her an innkeeper). **The text:** her house is built into the city wall. She hides two spies, lies to the king's men, and gives an assessment of the war more accurate than anything her own city has produced: "I know that the LORD has given you the land." She bargains for her whole household, and the spies swear an oath and tell her to tie a scarlet cord in her window. When the city falls they bring her family out, "and she has lived in Israel to this day." The New Testament remembers her three times. **Dominion:** POSSIBLE: a conversion story, the fallen woman who chose correctly, never a woman who conducted foreign policy from the wall. **Breaks:** Rahab's intelligence is believed. Ask whether yours would be.
Ruth · the book of Ruth | **Reduced to:** a wedding reading. **The text:** a Moabite widow with no obligation to anyone, who binds herself to her mother-in-law in the most famous vow of devotion in scripture, spoken by a woman to a woman. She gleans as a foreign laborer under the harvest-edge law. Naomi sends her to the threshing floor to uncover Boaz's feet and wait, "and he will tell you what to do." Ruth doesn't wait. She tells him to spread his cloak over her, the same word he'd used for the wings of the God she'd sheltered under. When the son is born, the women say "a son has been born to Naomi," and that Ruth is better to her than seven sons. Deuteronomy 23.3 bars Moabites from the assembly, and the book writes one into the royal line. **Dominion:** POSSIBLE: the model immigrant who assimilates completely, the threshing floor retold as modesty and the vow given to a husband. **Breaks:** Ruth's gamble works because one man chose to honor a law he could have ignored.
corr: The old companion said Rahab's deal was "in writing." It's an oath and a scarlet cord, and the text doesn't say her house was left standing. It also said Ruth proposed on Naomi's coaching. Naomi told her Boaz would tell her what to do, and Ruth spoke first.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · VIII–X" m="1 SAM 1–2; 1 SAM 25; 1 SAM 18–19; 2 SAM 3.13–16; 2 SAM 6.16–23; B. MEGILLAH 14A; LUKE 1.46–55; TKC: TL · WR"
# Hannah · Abigail · Michal
lede: Three women of the early monarchy, and the three ways the record lets a woman speak to power.
rec:
Hannah · 1 Samuel 1–2 | One of two wives, provoked for years by the one with children. Her husband asks, "Am I not more to you than ten sons?" She prays at the sanctuary with her lips moving and no sound, and the priest thinks she's drunk. She corrects him. She vows her son to God's service, leaves him at Shiloh after weaning, and brings him a little robe every year. Her song says the LORD raises the poor from the dust and brings down the mighty, and it's the model for Mary's song. It ends with the first mention in the Bible's order of a king as God's "anointed," from a woman praying before Israel has a king.
Abigail · 1 Samuel 25 | Married to Nabal, a rich man called harsh. David, an outlaw with six hundred men, asks for provisions in return for having left Nabal's shepherds unharmed. INTERPRETATION: Many readers hear a protection payment. Nabal refuses, and David arms four hundred men to kill every male in the house. Abigail loads donkeys with food and rides out without telling her husband. She kneels, takes the blame on herself, and talks David out of blood guilt he'd regret as king. Nabal dies ten days later, and David marries her. The Talmud counts her among seven women prophets.
Michal · 1 Samuel 18–19, 2 Samuel 3, 6 | Saul's daughter. The narrator says she loved David, a thing the Hebrew Bible rarely says of a woman. She saves his life by letting him down through a window and putting an idol in his bed. Saul gives her to another man, Palti. David later demands her back as a political condition, and Palti follows her weeping until he's ordered home. When David dances before the ark she despises him, and the chapter ends: "Michal the daughter of Saul had no child to the day of her death."
box: For TKC
Hannah is House Mother of House Aurem (TL). "Six Trials. Twelve girls. None of them hers" (TL). Abigail is the House's Debate and Protocol Advisor, "pressure in a pretty dress" (WR 30). POSSIBLE: Hannah's story runs the biblical one in reverse, a woman deciding whether to give her daughter Naomi to the institution she's served (FR 29).
/end
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XI–XII" m="2 SAM 11–12; 2 SAM 13; 1 KGS 1–2; MATT 1.6; TRIBLE, TEXTS OF TERROR (1984)"
# Bathsheba · Tamar of the palace
lede: What the court does to a woman it has already harmed.
note: Sexual violence and murder.
rec:
Bathsheba · 2 Samuel 11–12, 1 Kings 1–2 | **Reduced to:** a temptress on a roof. **The text:** she's purifying herself after her period, and the text doesn't say where. Every verb in the scene belongs to the king: he saw, he sent, he took. Her only words are "I am pregnant," two words in Hebrew. Her husband is killed on David's written order, carried in his own hand. Nathan's parable makes her the poor man's lamb. Her first child dies. Decades later she works with Nathan to put her son on the throne and sits at his right hand as queen mother. Matthew names her only as "the wife of Uriah." **Dominion:** POSSIBLE: keeps the roof and drops the verbs. A woman's visible body explains a powerful man's decision, the most useful doctrine a court can have. **Breaks:** Bathsheba survives into power. Ask what that costs, and what she becomes.
Tamar · 2 Samuel 13 | **Reduced to:** nothing. Most people raised in church have never heard of her. **The text:** a princess sent by her father's own instruction to her half-brother's sickroom, and raped there. Before it she argues, tells him "such a thing is not done in Israel," and offers a lawful alternative. Afterward she tears the robe worn by the king's virgin daughters, puts ashes on her head and goes away crying aloud. Her full brother tells her to be quiet. Her father is very angry and does nothing. She lives "a desolate woman" in her brother's house, and that's the last line she gets. Absalom later names his own daughter Tamar. **Dominion:** POSSIBLE: never read aloud. A king burying his daughter's assault to protect the succession isn't survivable material for a dynasty. **Breaks:** Tamar's outcry changes nothing. If your character's does, you've chosen mercy. Earn it.
corr: The old companion placed Bathsheba bathing "in her own home" and gave her first words as five. The text doesn't give the place, and the words are two in Hebrew.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XIII–XVI" m="1 KGS 16.31; 1 KGS 21; 2 KGS 9.30–37; 2 KGS 11; 2 KGS 22.14–20; ESTH 1–2; REV 2.20; WHITE, AR'N'T I A WOMAN? (1985); TKC: DOM · FR 33"
# Jezebel · Athaliah · Huldah · Vashti
lede: Four women with public power, and four ways the record handles it.
rec:
Jezebel · 1 Kings 16–21, 2 Kings 9 | A Sidonian princess married to Ahab who supports Baal worship and has the LORD's prophets killed. When Naboth won't sell his vineyard, she writes letters in Ahab's name, seals them with his seal and arranges false witnesses so Naboth is executed by correct procedure. The crime is real. Her death comes in 2 Kings 9. She paints her eyes, calls down to the usurper from her window and is thrown out of it. **Afterlife:** the name became an insult for any woman with influence, and under slavery and Jim Crow "Jezebel" named a stereotype of Black women as sexually predatory, used to excuse their abuse. The insult makes power the sin, more than the murder.
Athaliah · 2 Kings 11 | The only woman to rule Judah in her own name, for six years. She takes the throne by killing the royal family, as several male kings took theirs. An infant prince survives because his aunt Jehosheba hides him in the temple. In the seventh year a priest crowns the boy, and Athaliah cries "Treason! Treason!" before she's killed. Her reign is recorded as usurpation, and the same conduct in men as accession.
Huldah · 2 Kings 22 | A prophet in Jerusalem. When the law scroll is found in the temple, King Josiah sends the high priest, the royal secretary and three other officials to inquire of the LORD, and they go to her. Jeremiah and Zephaniah were active, and nobody explains why they chose her or remarks that she's a woman. She authenticates the scroll and gives an oracle, and the greatest reform in the kingdom's history follows. **Dominion:** POSSIBLE: not in the syllabus at any level. The biblical Huldah has no connection to the Prelate, whose working name is Aurelia (POSSIBLE, FR 33).
Vashti · Esther 1 | Ordered to appear in her crown before drunk officials, she refuses. The advisers panic about precedent, since every wife will hear of it. She's deposed by decree, and letters go out that every man is to be master in his own house. The plot of the book runs on her empty place.
corr: The old companion cited 1 Kings for Jezebel's death. It's 2 Kings 9. It also said Vashti is "never mentioned again." The king remembers her in 2.1, and Esther is made queen "instead of Vashti."
prompt: Which of these four is in Dominion's syllabus, and how she's taught | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XVII–XVIII" m="ESTH 2–9; ESTH 4.14–16; ESTH 9.29–32; PROV 31; RUTH 3.11; TKC: DR3 · TR"
# Esther · the woman of worth
rec:
Esther · the book of Esther | Hadassah, an orphan raised by her cousin, is "taken" in a royal search for young virgins. Twelve months of beauty treatment, six with oil of myrrh and six with spices, then one night with the king. She hides her people on her cousin's instruction. When Haman wins a decree to destroy the Jews, Mordecai says "Who knows whether you have not come to the kingdom for such a time as this?" Approaching the king unsummoned is punishable by death, and she goes: "If I perish, I perish." She works through two banquets, exposes Haman and wins a counter-decree. In chapter 9 the Jews kill their attackers, seventy-five thousand in the provinces. She then writes with full authority to fix Purim, so the book ends with her issuing a decree. God isn't named anywhere in the Hebrew. The Assessment in Draft Three uses "Esther's crown" as proof a woman could influence government "so long as she was beautiful, obedient, and prepared to die for the privilege" (DR3).
The woman of worth · Proverbs 31 | An acrostic poem. *ʾEshet ḥayil* is usually "virtuous" or "capable" wife, and *ḥayil* also means an army's strength and wealth. She buys a field and plants a vineyard with her own earnings, makes garments and sells them to merchants, "opens her mouth with wisdom," and her husband is known at the gates because of her. The chapter opens with what King Lemuel's mother taught him: stay off wine when judging, and "open your mouth for the mute… defend the rights of the poor and needy." Boaz uses the same phrase for Ruth. Verse 29 is printed on the Assessment and read before the scores (DR3). In Jewish practice it's sung at the Friday evening table.
box: For TKC
The Trials are a state search for young women with a long preparation (TR). POSSIBLE: The Temple teaches Vashti right before Esther as the warning, and never teaches that Esther ends the book issuing a decree in her own name.
/end
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XIX" m="GEN 19.26; JUDG 11.29–40; JUDG 19–21; 2 KGS 4.1–37; 2 KGS 8.1–6; TRIBLE, TEXTS OF TERROR"
# The unnamed, and two widows
note: The woman of Judges 19, sexual violence and dismemberment. Jephthah's daughter, a child sacrifice.
rec:
Lot's wife · Genesis 19 | Looks back at Sodom and becomes a pillar of salt. No name.
Jephthah's daughter · Judges 11 | Her father vows to sacrifice whatever comes out of his house first if he wins. She comes out dancing. She asks for two months in the mountains with her companions to mourn, he carries out the vow, and "it became a custom" for the daughters of Israel to lament her four days a year. No name.
The woman of Judges 19 | A Levite's secondary wife. At Gibeah men surround the house demanding the guest, and the Levite pushes her outside. She's raped all night and found at dawn with her hands on the threshold. He cuts her body into twelve pieces and sends them through Israel. The civil war that follows ends with the abduction of the women of Jabesh-gilead and the dancing girls of Shiloh. "Everyone did what was right in his own eyes." No name.
The prophet's widow · 2 Kings 4 | A creditor is about to take her two children as slaves. Elisha tells her to borrow every empty jar, shut the door and pour her one jar of oil. It runs until the jars do. She pays the debt and lives on the rest. The miracle keeps two children out of debt slavery.
The Shunammite · 2 Kings 4, 8 | A wealthy woman who builds Elisha a room. When her son dies she rides to the prophet and won't leave without him. Years later she comes home from a famine to find her land taken, petitions the king herself, and gets it back with the income it earned while she was gone.
prompt: Which unnamed woman in the Empire's records I'll give a name to | fill

@page id="p2-ntwomen" rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XX" m="LUKE 1–2; LUKE 1.34–38; LUKE 1.46–55; JOHN 2.1–5; MARK 3.21, 31–35; JOHN 19.25–27; ACTS 1.14; ISA 7.14; TKC: DOM"
# Mary of Nazareth
small: Luke 1–2 · Mark 3 · John 2 · 19 · Acts 1 · a young woman who sings about thrones emptied
rec:
Usually reduced to | Serenity. A statue in blue, silent and compliant, the whole personality reduced to one word of consent.
What the text gives | Told she'll bear a son, she asks a practical question, "How will this be?", before she agrees. She goes "with haste" to Elizabeth's house in the hill country and stays three months, and the song she sings there takes Hannah's as its model: God has brought down the mighty from their thrones, filled the hungry and sent the rich away empty, all in the past tense, as though already done. At Cana she tells him there's no wine and tells the servants to do whatever he says. In Mark the family comes to take him home because people say he's out of his mind. She stands at the cross in John and is with the disciples after.
CONTESTED | Matthew quotes Isaiah 7.14 from the Greek, "a virgin shall conceive." The Hebrew *ʿalmah* means a young woman of marriageable age, and the translation is one of the oldest disputes between Jewish and Christian readers. Luke never gives her age. INFERENCE: The usual "teenager" comes from ancient marriage customs.
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: Builds a doctrine of womanhood on her single yes and sets the song so gently nobody hears the words. The revolution she sings is footnoted as spiritual.
Where it breaks | Her consent is asked for. An angel waits for an answer. At an Aurem Binding, "nobody asks if she consents. They ask if she is worthy" (DOM). INTERPRETATION: Every Binding held in a Temple that honors Mary contradicts the story it honors.
corr: The old companion said she "travels alone" to Elizabeth's. The text doesn't say whether anyone went with her.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XXI–XXII" m="LUKE 8.1–3; JOHN 20.11–18; LUKE 24.10–11; GREGORY THE GREAT, HOMILY 33 (591); JOHN 4.1–42; TKC: TR"
# Mary Magdalene · the woman at the well
lede: Two women sent to speak, and what was said about them afterward.
rec:
Mary Magdalene · all four gospels | **Reduced to:** a prostitute. The text never says so. **The text:** from Magdala. Seven demons had gone out of her, and she and other women supported Jesus and the twelve "out of their own means." She's at the cross and the burial in all four and named first in most lists of the women. In John she's the first to see the risen Jesus and is sent to tell the disciples, "I have seen the Lord." In Luke the apostles think the women's report is nonsense. **Afterlife:** Pope Gregory the Great merged her with the unnamed sinful woman of Luke 7 and with Mary of Bethany in a sermon in 591, and it stuck for fourteen centuries. The Catholic calendar dropped the identification in 1969 and in 2016 raised her memorial to a feast with the old title "apostle to the apostles." **TKC:** POSSIBLE: The Broadcast's Eve narrative "implies and never accuses" (TR), and the reputations it assigns outlast the Trial.
The woman at the well · John 4 | **Reduced to:** her five husbands. **The text:** a Samaritan woman at noon. Jesus asks her for a drink, and she asks why a Jewish man is asking a Samaritan woman. Their conversation is the longest Jesus has with any one person in the gospels. She raises the live dispute between their peoples, Gerizim or Jerusalem, and gets a serious answer. He says she's had five husbands, and the text doesn't explain or condemn it. She's the first person in John to whom he says plainly he's the Messiah. She leaves her water jar, and "many Samaritans believed because of the woman's testimony." She's never given a name. **Breaks:** she gets a real answer. Ask who in Dominion would answer her.
corr: The old companion said "women in that world did not divorce men." Under Jewish law only a husband could start a divorce, and Roman law let either spouse. The text doesn't say how her marriages ended.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XXIII–XXIV" m="MARK 5.25–34; LEV 15.25–30; MARK 14.3–9; LUKE 10.38–42; JOHN 11.27; JOHN 12.1–8"
# The bleeding woman, the anointer, Martha and Mary
rec:
The woman who bled twelve years · Mark 5 | She's spent everything on doctors and gotten worse. She comes up behind him in a crowd and touches his cloak. Under Leviticus 15 she'd count as impure. He stops and asks who touched him, and she comes forward trembling and tells "the whole truth." He calls her "Daughter." The story is set inside the raising of a twelve-year-old girl. INTERPRETATION: She takes what she needs without permission and is commended for it.
The woman who anointed him · Mark 14 | Two days before his arrest an unnamed woman breaks a jar of costly ointment over his head. The men scold her for the waste, and he says wherever the gospel is preached, what she's done will be told in memory of her. Her name wasn't kept.
Martha and Mary of Bethany · Luke 10, John 11–12 | In Luke, Mary sits at his feet, the posture of a disciple, while Martha is "distracted with much serving." He says Mary has chosen the good portion. In John, Martha makes the confession Peter makes in the other gospels: "You are the Christ, the Son of God." Mary anoints his feet and wipes them with her hair.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XXV–XXX" m="ROM 16.1–7; ACTS 18.24–26; ACTS 16.11–15, 40; ACTS 9.36–43; ACTS 6.1–6; 1 TIM 5.3–16; EPP, JUNIA (2005); TKC: DOM"
# The women who ran things
lede: Named, titled and working, in the same letters used to silence women. Any institution has to choose which list is the rule.
rec:
Phoebe · Romans 16 | A *diakonos* of the church at Cenchreae, the word translated "deacon" for men, and a *prostatis*, a patron, "of many and of myself as well." INFERENCE: She carried the letter to Rome, and couriers often read letters aloud and explained them. The KJV and ESV call her a "servant." The NRSV and NIV (2011) say "deacon."
Junia · Romans 16 | Greeted with Andronicus as "of note among the apostles," and as Paul's fellow prisoners. Early writers including John Chrysostom read her as a woman apostle. From the Middle Ages many editions printed a masculine "Junias," a name with no ancient attestation. Eldon Epp traces the change (2005). CONTESTED: "Prominent among the apostles" or "well known to the apostles."
Priscilla · Acts 18, Romans 16 | A tentmaker with her husband Aquila, with a church in their house. When the gifted preacher Apollos arrives knowing only John's baptism, they take him aside and explain "the way of God more accurately." She's named first in four of six mentions.
Lydia · Acts 16 | A dealer in purple cloth, a luxury trade, with her own household and no husband mentioned. The first person baptized in Philippi. She insists the missionaries stay with her, and after prison they go back to her house to meet the believers. The patron.

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WOMEN · XXV–XXX, CONTINUED" m="ROM 16.1–7; ACTS 18.24–26; ACTS 16.11–15, 40; ACTS 9.36–43; ACTS 6.1–6; 1 TIM 5.3–16; EPP, JUNIA (2005); TKC: DOM"
# The women who ran things, continued
rec:
Tabitha · Acts 9 | The only person in the New Testament called *mathētria*, the feminine of "disciple." When she dies, the widows show Peter the garments she'd made. Mutual aid, recorded as ministry.
The widows · Acts 6, 1 Timothy 5 | The first recorded dispute in Jerusalem is over Greek-speaking widows neglected in the daily food. 1 Timothy sets up an enrolled order of widows, age sixty and up, with support.
box: For TKC
POSSIBLE: Titles like deacon and patron are rendered as domestic words in some translations, and the Empire's designations do the same on purpose. Tabitha's widows look like Irongate's Kitchen and Meridian's Reading Circles (DOM).
/end
corr: The old companion said Junia was "imprisoned for it," for being an apostle. The text says only that she'd been in prison with Paul.
prompt: Who is my Phoebe, my Lydia, my Junia | fill

@page id="p2-men" rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · MEN · I–V" m="GEN 12–25; GEN 18.22–33; GEN 25–35; GEN 37–50; GEN 44.18–34; EXOD 2.11–15; EXOD 7.7; ACTS 7.23, 30; KIERKEGAARD, FEAR AND TREMBLING (1843)"
# The founders
lede: The founders aren't presented as moral exemplars. They're presented, with startling candor, as men whose obedience keeps endangering the people in their care.
rec:
Abraham · Genesis 12–25 | Held up as the model of faith. The text records him twice passing his wife off as his sister to protect himself, sending Hagar and Ishmael into the wilderness, and taking his son up a mountain with a knife. It also records him bargaining with God over Sodom, from fifty righteous down to ten: "Shall not the Judge of all the earth do what is just?" Paul reads him as justified by faith, James by works. POSSIBLE: Dominion preaches the mountain and never the bargaining.
Jacob · Genesis 25–35 | Buys his brother's birthright for stew and takes the blessing by disguise. Deceived in turn on his wedding night, he works twenty years under shifting terms. He wrestles all night, is renamed Israel, "strives with God," and limps afterward. He loves Rachel over Leah and Joseph over his brothers, openly. **For TKC:** the favoritism engine.
Judah · Genesis 37–38, 43–44 | Leah's fourth son. He proposes selling Joseph instead of killing him, wrongs Tamar and admits it, and in Egypt offers himself as a slave in Benjamin's place, in the longest speech in Genesis. His name became the tribe's, the kingdom's and finally the word "Jew."
Joseph · Genesis 37–50 | Sold, imprisoned on a false accusation, and raised to run Egypt's grain. His famine policy ends with the population selling its land and itself to the crown. "You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good." **For TKC:** the competent administrator whose competence builds the machine.
Moses · Exodus–Deuteronomy | Raised in the palace of the regime killing his people. He kills an overseer and runs to Midian. He argues with God five times before he goes and later talks God out of destroying the people. He's eighty when he faces Pharaoh. Barred from the land after striking the rock, he dies in sight of it, in a grave no one knows.
corr: The old companion said Moses spent "forty years as a shepherd." Exodus doesn't give the length. The forty years come from Stephen's speech (Acts 7) and later tradition.
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · MEN · VI" m="1 SAM 16 – 1 KGS 2; 1 SAM 13.14; 1 SAM 25; 2 SAM 6; 2 SAM 7; 2 SAM 11–13; 1 KGS 2.5–9; TKC: TL"
# David
small: 1 Samuel 16 – 1 Kings 2 · the man every scriptural monarchy has claimed as precedent
rec:
Usually reduced to | A boy with a sling, a psalmist with a harp, and "a man after God's own heart." Three images, all from the first third of his story.
What the text gives | The most fully drawn character in the Hebrew Bible, and the portrait isn't flattering. A gifted musician and a brilliant political operator. An outlaw whose men ask Nabal for payment. A king who dances before the ark and quarrels with Michal over it. A man who sees a woman from a roof, sends for her, and has her husband killed by written order. A father who does nothing when his son rapes his daughter, and nearly loses the kingdom to another son. On his deathbed he gives his heir a list of men to deal with.
What Dominion does | POSSIBLE: Everything. Divinely chosen, anointed by a prophet, forgiven after private repentance and promised an eternal dynasty (2 Sam 7), the legal basis of every throne that ever called itself Christian. His crimes are taught as a lesson in personal repentance and never as a case for accountability.
TKC echoes | The Emperor, and the founding myth he's measured against. Judah, if he's being trained into a role built on a man whose worst acts are on the record and forgiven in advance.
Where it breaks | A prophet walks into the throne room, tells him what he did, and lives. That scene is the difference between a monarchy with a conscience mechanism and one without. OPEN QUESTION: Does anyone in Dorian's court have Nathan's office?
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · MEN · VII–XIII" m="1 SAM 8.11–18; 2 SAM 12; 1 KGS 1–11; 1 KGS 9.20–22; 1 KGS 12; 1 KGS 17–19; JUDG 6–9; JER 26; JER 36; JER 38.7–13; EXOD 32; LEV 10"
# Kingmakers, prophets and priests
lede: The oldest fight in the collection. The priest is appointed, hereditary and salaried. The prophet is called, unlicensed and unpaid.
rec:
Samuel | When the people ask for a king "like all the nations," he warns them what a king will take: sons for his chariots, daughters for his kitchens, the best fields, a tenth of the flocks, "and you shall be his slaves." He anoints one anyway, and later anoints the replacement while the first is still alive. The cleric who makes rulers and outlives them.
Nathan | Delivers the dynastic promise, then tells David the story of the rich man and the poor man's lamb and says "You are the man." He survives, and later works with Bathsheba to put Solomon on the throne.
Solomon | Wins the succession and has rivals killed. His first judgment is between two women who are prostitutes, each claiming a baby, and he hears them both in person. He builds the temple with conscripted labor. 1 Kings 9 says the forced labor fell on non-Israelites, and 1 Kings 12 says the north revolted over his "heavy yoke," so the passages pull against each other. He breaks all four prohibitions of Deuteronomy 17.
Gideon | Offered hereditary rule, he refuses: "The LORD will rule over you." Then he makes a gold ephod that becomes a snare. His son Abimelech kills seventy brothers to be king, and the survivor Jotham answers with the fable of the trees, where the olive, fig and vine all decline the crown and the bramble accepts it.

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · MEN · VII–XIII, CONTINUED" m="1 SAM 8.11–18; 2 SAM 12; 1 KGS 1–11; 1 KGS 9.20–22; 1 KGS 12; 1 KGS 17–19; JUDG 6–9; JER 26; JER 36; JER 38.7–13; EXOD 32; LEV 10"
# Kingmakers, prophets and priests, continued
rec:
Elijah | Confronts Ahab and Jezebel and wins on Carmel. Then he runs, asks to die, and is fed and told to sleep. God comes to him in "a sound of thin silence." Malachi promises his return. The King James New Testament spells him Elias.
Jeremiah | Tried for his life, his scroll cut up and burned, dropped in a muddy cistern. A Cushite palace servant, Ebed-melech, gets the king's permission and pulls him out with ropes and rags.
Aaron | The first high priest. He makes the calf, announces a feast to the LORD and says the gold went into the fire "and out came this calf." He keeps his office, joins Miriam's challenge and isn't struck.
box: The structural point for Dominion
POSSIBLE: When Dominion folded the prophet into the priest, it didn't abolish prophecy. It made prophecy an employment category, a far more elegant solution.
/end
prompt: Questions for the novel | fill

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · MEN · XIV–XVIII" m="MARK 8.32–33; JOHN 21.15–17; GAL 2.11–14; GAL 3.28; 1 COR 14.34–35; JOHN 12.6; MATT 26.14–16; MATT 27.24; JOSEPHUS, ANT. 18.116–119; PILATE INSCRIPTION, CAESAREA (1961)"
# The men of the New Testament
lede: The men closest to the center are shown getting it wrong again and again, and the record was kept by the movement they went on to lead.
rec:
Peter | Rebukes Jesus and is called "Satan." Cuts off a servant's ear in the garden. Denies knowing him three times, the first to a servant girl. Restored over breakfast with a question asked three times. Paul rebukes him in public at Antioch for withdrawing from gentile tables.
Paul | A Pharisee who persecuted the movement and joined it. Imprisoned repeatedly, he writes that in Christ there is "no longer slave or free, no longer male and female," and greets women leaders by name.
Judas | Keeps the common purse. The gospels give different reasons for what he does. Matthew has him ask what they'll pay, John calls him a thief, Luke and John say Satan entered him. Matthew and Acts give two different deaths.
Pilate | The Roman prefect of Judea, 26–36 CE. All four gospels have him find no case and order the execution anyway. Only Matthew has him wash his hands. An inscription found at Caesarea in 1961 names him. **For TKC:** POSSIBLE: the Crown advisor in the Pairing Introduction (TR) is a Pilate.
John the Baptist | A priest's son preaching in the wilderness, who calls the crowds a "brood of vipers." Herod Antipas has him beheaded at a banquet. The gospels give a personal reason, his objection to Herod's marriage. Josephus gives a political one, Herod's fear of his hold on the crowds.
corr: The old companion said the silencing verses and Galatians 3.28 are "both his." 1 Corinthians 14.34–35 sits elsewhere in some manuscripts and may be a later addition, and 1 Timothy's authorship is CONTESTED. It also said "one gospel implies disillusionment" for Judas. None does.
prompt: Which of these men is Judah, and which is Dorian | fill

@page id="p2-cast" rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WHO ECHOES WHOM · 1 OF 3" m="TKC: TL · TR · FR 0 · FR 3 · FR 33; DECIDED"
# The names in the cast
Many of the cast carry biblical names. The table records what each name points to. Whether an echo is meant is your call, so every application after this page is POSSIBLE unless it carries a source tag.
tabrl:
| Name | Who in TKC | Biblical source |
| Eden Selah Osei | Covenant Daughter of Aurem (TL) | The garden (Gen 2–3). *Selah* is a liturgical marker of unknown meaning, seventy-one times in Psalms and three in Habakkuk. |
| Miriam Osei | Eden's mother (TL) | Miriam the prophet. Mariam in Greek, so every Mary in the New Testament. |
| Leah Osei | Eden's younger sister (TL, DECIDED) | Leah, the fertile, unloved wife (Gen 29–30) |
| Zion Osei | Eden's brother (TL) | A place: Jerusalem's hill, and the city addressed as "daughter Zion" |
| Solomon Osei | Eden's brother, sixteen (DECIDED) | Solomon, Bathsheba's son, born after the household broke |
| Thomas Osei | Eden's father, who left voluntarily (FR 3) | Thomas, who wouldn't believe without seeing (John 20) |
| Hannah | House Mother, House Aurem (TL) | Hannah, who gave her only son to the sanctuary |
| Naomi | Hannah's daughter (TL) | Naomi, who came home empty and planned Ruth's future |
| Abigail | Debate and Protocol Advisor (TL) | Abigail, who talked an armed man out of a massacre on her knees |

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WHO ECHOES WHOM · 1 OF 3, CONTINUED" m="TKC: TL · TR · FR 0 · FR 3 · FR 33; DECIDED"
# The names in the cast, continued
tabrl:
| Name | Who in TKC | Biblical source |
| Ruth Gideon | Covenant Daughter of Heartland (TR) | Ruth, and Gideon, who refused a crown and made a snare |
| Judah Elijah Vane | Crown Heir (TL) | Judah, who offered himself in his brother's place. Elijah, who confronted a king and queen. |
| Elias Vane | Founder (TL) | Elias is the King James New Testament spelling of Elijah |
| Zara Voss | Covenant Daughter of Sable (TR) | Zara is the King James spelling of Zerah, Tamar's twin |
| Sera Callum Vane · Sera Montoya | Elias's wife (TL) · Covenant Daughter of Pacific (TR) | Serah, daughter of Asher, one of the few daughters named in the genealogies |
| Petra Solano | Covenant Daughter of Mountain (TR) | A word: Greek *petra*, "rock" (Matt 16.18) |
| Azrael Vane | Dorian's elder son (TL) | Not biblical. The angel of death in later Islamic and Jewish tradition. |
small: SUPERSEDED: The timeline's age of thirteen for Solomon. DECIDED: Solomon is sixteen, and Leah is younger than Eden.

@page rh="THE PEOPLE OF THE TEXT · WHO ECHOES WHOM · 2 OF 3" m="TKC: TL · TR · DOM · DR3 · WR · AM · FR 26 · FR 29 · FR 41–42"
# Echoes, labeled
rec:
Seven women prophets | The Talmud's list (Megillah 14a) is Sarah, Miriam, Deborah, Hannah, Abigail, Huldah and Esther. Eden's mother, her House Mother and her protocol advisor all carry prophets' names. POSSIBLE: Celeste knows the list and has never said so.
Elias as Elijah | POSSIBLE: The Temple reads its canonized founder (TL) as the Elijah Malachi promised, and named the Crown Heir Judah Elijah to carry it. Elijah's story includes the collapse after the victory, and the Temple doesn't teach it.
Huldah | The ledger and the Covenant of Dissent are found documents (TL, FR 41–42). OPEN QUESTION: Who in the Empire would be asked to authenticate the Dissent if it surfaced?
Tamar's method | POSSIBLE: Eden's method in a world run by the Registry, and its limit. Tamar had a law on her side that wasn't enforced. Eden's law is against her.
Jezebel | Aurelia is built over Atlanta's Black memory, and Gulf candidates are penalized for warmth (DOM). POSSIBLE: "Jezebel" is the word the Broadcast never says on air and its editors use in the room.
Samuel 8 and Jotham's fable | Two anti-monarchy texts in the Empire's own canon. POSSIBLE: Pacific's grandmothers (DOM) know the bramble fable by heart.
Ebed-melech | POSSIBLE: Hannah, who knows the back staircases and the camera gaps (TL), is this kind of insider.
Jeremiah's scroll | Dorian removed the Covenant of Dissent (FR 42). POSSIBLE: Someone can still recite it, the way Celeste can recite the Covenant of Purpose (AM).
John the Baptist | INTERPRETATION: The Empire's "sudden medical event" for Azrael and Petra works the same way as the gospels' account of John, a private explanation for a public act.
prompt: The echo I'm keeping, and the one I'm cutting | fill

